ke take a quick look at  sort of some of the formats that we  already support for um four-way data  sets so as you can see you can use  whatever file formats you're typically  comfortable with or read things in  directly from other data frames um if  this diagram is a little hard to read i  would encourage you to check out the ray  datasets uh documentation which contains  these figures  um and we'll have a link to that at the  end of this talk  right cool  um yeah and so um you know it's not just  important to support these types of  operations we want to make sure we can  do them efficiently and easily so the  api that we want you to be able to use  should be as simple as just read parquet  or read csv  the exact type of thing you would be  used to using in arrow or panda  um  and we also want to make sure that you  can literally still convert to and from  pandas  um and you know like again you should  also be able to  write things out to storage just as  easily as you read them in  um  and again so we really leverage arrow  here and so that allows us to get really  really high performance on all of our  nodes and ray lets us parallelize this  easily and so you know even in  production already we've seen companies  like amazon that are using data sets for  um  ingesting large amounts of data and  we've seen that you know the  sort of performance we see um from arrow  on single threads we're able to scale  that up close to linearly to jobs that  are in the petabyte or tens of petabyte  range  all right cool so let's take a quick  look at some of the last mile  pre-processing operations that we  support um so  these are in a lot of ways some of the  operations that you would expect out of  most other libraries that you use for  pre-processing so you can do things like  mapping batching filtering you can do  some aggregations on your data  um and we also support some shuffle  operations which if you come from more  of a systems background you'll recognize  these as s