th a single line of code  another really important uh  concept that we support is windowing and  so windowing essentially  is best described as you can imagine  what would happen if you tried to pass  your entire data through a pipeline at  once  you'll find that you need to be able to  store you know all your data and a lot  of your intermediate results  in your cluster at a single time  so this data is really really large you  either need a massive potentially  infeasibly large cluster or you might  have to take a performance hit by  spilling the disk  and so windowing is a concept where we  can break our data set up into chunks  that all fit in our cluster  and then pass our pass each window in  one at a time through our pipeline  and this will allow us to keep  everything in memory um to keep  everything really efficient while uh not  having to spill to disk  right cool so now i'm going to pass it  on to clark who's going to talk a little  bit about the different use cases that  people have with ml datasets and take a  look at a couple of real world examples  of how people are using it today  thanks alex first awesome first half of  the talk um my name is clark i'm on  alex's team and i focus on a lot of like  data plane stuff and i've been working  on data sets a lot recently so the two  big use cases that we want to talk about  that data sets really excels at is  scalable shuffled ml ingest and  efficient batch inference  scalable shuffled ml ingest first if  you're not familiar a little bit of  background  what exactly do we mean by ml ingest  fundamentally this is just loading data  into one or more model trainers  which on a single node can be pretty  simple but once you go into distributed  setting things get a little bit more  complex but first a quick little  overview of uh what exactly is involved  in ml ingests  uh typically you're going to be loading  the data from storage  you're then going to want to partition  the data into a shard per trainer if  