st  why exactly is this even a problem you  know aren't you just loading data into  your trainers like why  isn't this just simple ingest why is  this tough uh well first at a certain  data and cluster scale you need  parallelized scalable reading from  storage if you did it all serially with  a single thread  um your trainers would be super unhappy  because they'd be waiting forever  um and then you also need to uh  you need to try to not waste your idle  uh you you don't want to you don't want  your gpus to be idle while you're  reading and shuffling data as alex  talked about uh just because your gpu is  idle uh doesn't mean that you're not  paying for it  uh you're almost always paying for that  gpu that's just sitting there um so for  example here  uh this is a situation where a trainer  is waiting for data to be  loaded and shuffled before training on  each epoch and as you can see we have  all these gaps where the gpu is sitting  idle um so that's all time uh those are  all times when you're paying for a gpu  but it's not actually being used  and then also a few other things that  make this hard well first we have to  keep the api simple despite the fact  that we're now talking about  large-scale data  and we're also talking about large  clusters and distributed training we  still want the api to be as simple as  possible  um also the full pipeline may not fit in  memory as alex just talked about  when talking about windowing um your  entire working set for your ingestion  pipeline especially when you're doing  shuffling which will create intermediate  copies of your data set might not all  fit in your cluster's memory  local shuffles they hurt model accuracy  as we talked about before  compared to a fully global shuffle  but global pre-puck shuffling uh in a  distributed setting is fundamentally  pretty difficult because of this like  all to all communication uh basically  there's between the mappers and reducers  in a shuffle there is a ton of data  interchange and this can result in a lot  of uh transferring of data uh over the  network  which is not fun  so how does a dataset solve this well  first  as uh alex hinted at our i o layer is  super scalable uh very easy to  parallelize reading from like high  bandwidth storage solutions like s3 and  hdfs for example that's all you have to  do right there  to launch 20 parallel reading tasks from  s3 it's a very simple api but a very  powerful io layer under the hood  um and just to give you an idea of what  this would look like  three parallel read tasks from s3 you  can feed your data to the trainers  pretty quickly  um also like how exactly do we keep the  gpu from sitting up idle how do we keep  the gpu saturated uh with data sets we  use the pipelining of the pre-processing  and shuffling stages with training  and uh instead of having these like  little gaps of gpu idle time you will  instead see that by pipelining the  shuffle data loading stage with the  model training stage we are able to  limit that gpu idle time just to pretty  much the first epoch after that first  epoch uh since we're concurrently uh  loading and shuffling the data uh for  the next epoch while training on the  kearney epoch uh you'll see that uh the  trainers never really have to wait for  the data loading and shuffling to finish  and as long as your shuffle data loading  throughput is uh is more than your  training throughput  then uh basically after the first epoch  the trainer should only see hot shuffle  data um and then per epoch shuffling  it's a difficult distributed problem how  do we do it efficiently well as alex  talked about our great distributed in  memory object store allows us to do  super efficient transfers and also zero  copy reads for workers that are on the  same node it allows us to do these  shuffle operations the map and reduce  stages very quickly  we also have locality replacement of  shuffle shards onto trainers uh and  that's as simple in this api example uh  where w