umber of  epochs that you want you should make  sure that each window is shuffled and  then you feed that data set to the  trainer for it to consume and train on  to extend this to a large data scale and  a multi-node distributed training  setting this is really all that's  required right here  you window to constrain the working set  size and you split the data into shards  and feed those uh to trainers  um so case studies uh first a little bit  of background common problem here is  like the existing solution as a  bottleneck in their training pipeline um  so this is where shuffled data loading  is really the issue why they're not  training as fast as they want and then  also the models are shuffle sensitive  where the shuffle quality does in fact  affect model accuracy  we have two case studies here a  high-tech ml platform that's a startup  that's currently in stealth mode their  old solution was actually writing data  to s3 and then using petastorm to load  it into horabad  for those that don't know pedostorm is  an ingest library from uber  our solution that we pitched was porting  the pandas uh  to s3 uh portion of the pipeline to das  unrave preprocessing and then feeding  that directly to raid data sets for  ingestion into the horva trainers  and then the other case study was a  large tech company in transport space  that was originally loading data from s3  again using petastorm and we actually  said continue loading data from s3 but  this time use data sets uh both case  studies suffered from the above common  problems by the way like both of these  have the same issues  um so uh for benchmark results uh very  quick summary uh ray uh desk and raiden  datasets was eight times faster than the  old solution on a popular new york city  taxi data set and also uh for the other  case study of where we're still loading  from s3 uh we were four times faster  and this is by the way uh when we're  doing more work because we're doing a  global shuffle instead of a local  shuffle so you're getting a higher  quality shuffle and better performance  even at small scales  and then we're also working on more  comprehensive benchmarks that are in  progress  um the other use case i'll go over very  quickly is batch inference where you do  some pre-processing and then you perform  model inference over uh batches of data  and save the results  um so there are a few issues here there  are some scheduling issues you don't  want to transfer the data around the  cluster and you want to reserve gps for  a fraction of the time and you need to  keep the gpu saturated as we talked  about before  um we saw the scheduling issue with  raise grade scheduling properties  automatic data locality and raised  resource scheduling  and uh we use pipelining to solve the  issue of idle gpus  and the  case studies we have a startup that's  processing drone imagery and a high-tech  ml platform that's uh got an existing  solution uh just using pandas to torch  and the results  uh just to skip through it we're five  times faster on this data set for batch  entrance  um as you can see from these graphs here  and so we provide more resource  efficiency and better performance even  at small data scales  uh so in summary existing ml platforms  our ml pipelines suffer from a lot of  issues and raid data sets  we hit that mark  using uh hyperscale implementations of  ml operations like shuffled um we keep  the api simple and for mlm.js and batch  inference we  beat the existing competition by a lot  in terms of performance  so yeah i had to go through the end  there pretty quickly but uh yeah thank  you for listening to the talk and then  uh we're probably a little bit out of  time for questions but please hit us up  on our discussion forum uh check us out  online and uh we're hiring so if these  problems are interesting you please come  check us out

