 overhead of just copying around the  same data between different processes to  actually parallelize your your  operations  and finally there's this giant overhead  here where we wrote all of our data to a  storage system and we didn't really need  to do that right when we think about the  pipeline we think we want to do  pre-processing and then feed the data  into training  there was no  there's usually no hard requirement to  actually be writing the state of the  storage which is a very expensive  operation since you're changing the  format you need to write it to a disk um  etc  um and then there's also a lot of like  implementation and operational  complexity i think uh with with these  pipelines so you might have multiple  languages um you these different types  of pre-processing versus training  operations tend to be completely  different workloads that require  different types of scheduling and  different types of optimization  and they use different types of  resources so pre-processing is typically  done on large cpu clusters whereas for  training especially in the deep learning  world we typically want to use gpus  and then finally there are a lot of  operations that people in the machine  learning and research communities really  like and want to be able to use but the  current distributed systems out there  for doing uh you know machine learning  in production don't support so one  really big one here is per epoch  shuffling you know anytime you're doing  something with deep learning it's really  nice to be able to shuffle your data  every epoch to increase your model's  accuracy but there's no really good way  of doing that once you scale to multiple  machines today  all right so now we're going to talk a  little bit about ray data sets and uh  hopefully you'll see how ray data sets  solves a lot of these problems  so before we jump into it we're going to  take a quick look at ray so i don't want  to focus too much on on rey in this talk  but i do think it's worth mentioning  some of the really important properties  of ray that led us to build data sets on  top of it  because we we can really leverage ray to  do a lot of the heavy lifting to solve  some of those problems  so one of the most important things um  that ray provides us here is a really  efficient data layer so ray is largely  implemented in c plus and so  it's able to sort of bypass a lot of the  issues that python processes typically  have by using shared memory um to share  memory between different processes on  the same machine which allows us to  avoid doing a lot of copying  um  uh ray also is really well integrated  with arrow and so we're able to do  things like zero copy reads that avoid  having to deserialize your data um once  you read it into your process  you can just use that exact same  representation that you already had  stored in your object store  um ray also comes with locality aware  scheduling so if you have a large amount  of data you don't want to transfer it  around your cluster all the time  and so ray will try to make intelligent  scheduling decisions to uh do that and  then finally if you do really need to  move your data around the cluster this  is where you run into a lot of  distributed systems challenges usually  uh with overloading nodes and things  like that and ray comes with built-in  you know the object transfer protocols  are really built to handle these really  really stressful edge cases  and then the other really good benefit  of array here is that it's really  general purpose so it has a  [Music]  resource-based scheduler allow  allows you to specify the type of  resource that you want to give to  different types of types of tasks within  a single cluster and so this allows you  to do some things with cpus and other  things with gpus uh very seamlessly and  easily it's also extremely robust  and highly scalable so it can run  different types of workloads you can  even run an entire spark cluster on top  of your array cluster with very little  overhead  and uh it's very because it's very easy  to build distributed systems in rey  it really lets you access a wider  ecosystem of other libraries on top of  raid 2.  all right so now let's take a concrete  look at what ray datasets is so  fundamentally ray datasets provides  essentially a distributed table  abstraction  um  so this is very similar this  architecturally may seem very similar to  um frameworks you've already seen like  data frames like pi sparks data frame  for example um but we'll quickly go into  what makes them different but first i  want to talk about sort of what rate  data sets are designed to use so the  first and most important thing for ray  data sets is that it's meant to be an  easy way for you to get your data from  wherever you were doing a lot of your  heavy lifting etl type jobs  into array cluster whether that data is  coming directly from something like  spark or whether that data is coming  from something like s3 already like a  storage system  um we also want you to be able to do  some amount of pre-processing on that  data there's always these types of last  second like last mile pre-processing  operations that you may want to do  um right before you go into training  mode that it doesn't make sense to do  before  in earlier stages of your pre-processing  and then finally there are some types of  operations that you want to perform on  your data that require using a bunch of  different resources and these are things  where ray really shines and so we want  to enable you to do those types of  operations using a higher level api  using data sets  all right  so before we take a closer look uh one  thing i want to sort of emphasize here  is that ray datasets is not meant to be  a general purpose data frame library so  the sort of the place that we see data  sets uh fitting into the ecosystem if we  wanted to work in we want to make sure  it works well with the other data frame  libraries  so  um data sets in spark for example are  not meant to be the same thing what we  sort of envision you doing is if you  have a really large scale you know etl  job for uh getting your data into a  format to do  machine learning on we expect that  you'll still continue to use spark or  flink or whatever other um  whatever whatever other systems you're  using for etl  um and then use gray data datasets as  sort of that last thing that lets you  feed all that like data analytics um  type of you know formatted data into um  like a format that's usable for training  all right  so to take a closer look at this um how  we envision sort of or how we see ray  being used as a data loader is you  essentially start with your data in some  other format outside of your ray cluster  maybe that's hdfs or s3 maybe that's uh  existing data frame already  and we want rave data set to be sort of  this like  universal thing like a compatibility  layer that you pass it into  um and then we want to make sure that  all of the ml training libraries you use  are also compatible with data sets and  so  you may find today that it's very hard  for example to take something like spark  and pass the output of a large spark job  into something like tensorflow  um and so ray datasets is sort of  supposed to be um supposed to make that  possible and easy right so if you can  convert all of your etl data frames into  a ray data set you can do any last  second operations you need and then feed  that directly into your training  framework  and the way we really accomplish this  and are able to support so many things  quickly is by really leveraging arrow so  you know this may have all sounded  familiar to you because a lot of what  we've talked about is what arrow really  solves on a single node  and so data sets is meant to essentially  take what  play that role that error plays on a  single  machine and uh sort of provide you with  that same benefit in a distributed  setting  all right so um  here we can like take a quick look at  sort of some of the formats that we  already support for um four-way data  sets so as you can see you can use  whatever file formats you're typically  comfortable with or read things in  directly from other data frames um if  this diagram is a little hard to read i  would encourage you to check out the ray  datasets uh documentation which contains  these figures  um and we'll have a link to that at the  end of this talk  right cool  um yeah and so um you know it's not just  important to support these types of  operations we want to make sure we can  do them efficiently and easily so the  api that we want you to be able to use  should be as simple as just read parquet  or read csv  the exact type of thing you would be  used to using in arrow or panda  um  and we also want to make sure that you  can literally still convert to and from  pandas  um and you know like again you should  also be able to  write things out to storage just as  easily as you read them in  um  and again so we really leverage arrow  here and so that allows us to get really  really high performance on all of our  nodes and ray lets us parallelize this  easily and so you know even in  production already we've seen companies  like amazon that are using data sets for  um  ingesting large amounts of data and  we've seen that you know the  sort of performance we see um from arrow  on single threads we're able to scale  that up close to linearly to jobs that  are in the petabyte or tens of petabyte  range  all right cool so let's take a quick  look at some of the last mile  pre-processing operations that we  support um so  these are in a lot of ways some of the  operations that you would expect out of  most other libraries that you use for  pre-processing so you can do things like  mapping batching filtering you can do  some aggregations on your data  um and we also support some shuffle  operations which if you come from more  of a systems background you'll recognize  these as sort of the notoriously hard  operations that require a lot of  communication  um if you come from a machine learning  background these are what sort of the  operations that you just sort of expect  to have  and may be missing sometimes when you  use other systems  um so this is sort of just an example of  what data may look like as it passes  through your system so if you start with  some input you may want to do some  mapping on it  and then you want you might want to  shuffle that data around between your  different nodes so that all the data  that all the data ends up in the right  place before you reduce that data or do  something like training on that data  and then finally we'll aggregate that  output together maybe that's the output  of your model  all right  um and so yeah there's also a bunch of  other optimizations that we've built  into data sets um so for example we  support pipelining um and so pipelining  if you're unfamiliar with the concept is  a way of making sure that when we have  these really expensive clusters up we're  making the best use out of them  so if you imagine a typical pipeline um  you may have a loading step where you  load your data in you might want to do  some pre-processing and then you might  want to do some inference or score your  data  and if we naively did that  serially we would find that we would  start by loading our data and then we'd  pre-process then we do inference but  before the inference step started we  just had a bunch of gpus that we're  probably already paying for that are  sitting there idle doing nothing  and so the idea with pipelining is that  it would be great if once we loaded our  first bit of data we're able to begin to  do pre-processing on that immediately  and then being and do inference on that  immediately so we begin to use our gpu a  lot sooner and while we're using the gpu  we want to also be loading other data  and pre-processing other data in  parallel  and ray essentially lets you do this  with a single line of code  another really important uh  concept that we support is windowing and  so windowing essentially  is best described as you can imagine  what would happen if you tried to pass  your entire data through a pipeline at  once  you'll find that you need to be able to  store you know all your data and a lot  of your intermediate results  in your cluster at a single time  so this data is really really large you  either need a massive potentially  infeasibly large cluster or you might  have to take a performance hit by  spilling the disk  and so windowing is a concept where we  can break our data set up into chunks  that all fit in our cluster  and then pass our pass each window in  one at a time through our pipeline  and this will allow us to keep  everything in memory um to keep  everything really efficient while uh not  having to spill to disk  right cool so now i'm going to pass it  on to clark who's going to talk a little  bit about the different use cases that  people have with ml datasets and take a  look at a couple of real world examples  of how people are using it today  thanks alex first awesome first half of  the talk um my name is clark i'm on  alex's team and i focus on a lot of like  data plane stuff and i've been working  on data sets a lot recently so the two  big use cases that we want to talk about  that data sets really excels at is  scalable shuffled ml ingest and  efficient batch inference  scalable shuffled ml ingest first if  you're not familiar a little bit of  background  what exactly do we mean by ml ingest  fundamentally this is just loading data  into one or more model trainers  which on a single node can be pretty  simple but once you go into distributed  setting things get a little bit more  complex but first a quick little  overview of uh what exactly is involved  in ml ingests  uh typically you're going to be loading  the data from storage  you're then going to want to partition  the data into a shard per trainer if  you're doing distributed training  you're going to want to batch the data  into gpu batches  and then you're going to want to shuffle  the data before each epoch  and this is typically done using a local  shuffling where you only shuffle within  a trainer shard before each epoch  that's the most popular option  primarily because global shuffling uh  before each epoch is  more difficult but it actually ends up  being much better in order to achieve  like optimal model accuracy within a set  number of epochs and actually i'm going  to do a quick little aside on that uh  first for those that aren't familiar why  shuffle before an epoch like if we just  shuffle globally once before it's  training why isn't that fine  the tldr for this is that for certain  models uh epoch shuffling gives you much  better model accuracy  after a set number of epochs within a  given training window um and that's  often a common constraint of to users  that we've talked to where they have for  example a 12-hour window where they want  to train  as many epochs as possible so they care  about high training throughput and they  want to get as much precision gain  uh on uh each epoch  and  fundamentally if you want to know why  this is the case shuffling before each  epoch it decorates samples within and  across batches  um and uh in this case yeah we're doing  this before each epoch it reduces  variance it improves the generalization  of the model and it prevents uh it  prevents that pesky overfitting that we  all super hate when training our models  um and i i would go into a little more  detail but i think i'm really short on  time uh but fundamentally uh there are a  few papers out there that have examined  this um i  definitely would recommend uh one a  convergence and analysis of uh  stochastic gradient descent under  shuffling super great paper  um but generally results in an improved  statistical gain uh  for each step in the training process if  you shuffle before each epoch  um so first  why exactly is this even a problem you  know aren't you just loading data into  your trainers like why  isn't this just simple ingest why is  this tough uh well first at a certain  data and cluster scale you need  parallelized scalable reading from  storage if you did it all serially with  a single thread  um your trainers would be super unhappy  because they'd be waiting forever  um and then you also need to uh  you need to try to not waste your idle  uh you you don't want to you don't want  your gpus to be idle while you're  reading and shuffling data as alex  talked about uh just because your gpu is  idle uh doesn't mean that you're not  paying for it  uh you're almost always paying for that  gpu that's just sitting there um so for  example here  uh this is a situation where a trainer  is waiting for data to be  loaded and shuffled before training on  each epoch and as you can see we have  all these gaps where the gpu is sitting  idle um so that's all time uh those are  all times when you're paying for a gpu  but it's not actually being used  and then also a few other things that  make this hard well first we have to  keep the api simple despite the fact  that we're now talking about  large-scale data  and we're also talking about large  clusters and distributed training we  still want the api to be as simple as  possible  um also the full pipeline may not fit in  memory as alex just talked about  when talking about windowing um your  entire working set for your ingestion  pipeline especially when you're doing  shuffling which will create intermediate  copies of your data set might not all  fit in your cluster's memory  local shuffles they hurt model accuracy  as we talked about before  compared to a fully global shuffle  but global pre-puck shuffling uh in a  distributed setting is fundamentally  pretty difficult because of this like  all to all communication uh basically  there's between the mappers and reducers  in a shuffle there is a ton of data  interchange and this can result in a lot  of uh transferring of data uh over the  network  which is not fun  so how does a dataset solve this well  first  as uh alex hinted at our i o layer is  super scalable uh very easy to  parallelize reading from like high  bandwidth storage solutions like s3 and  hdfs for example that's all you have to  do right there  to launch 20 parallel reading tasks from  s3 it's a very simple api but a very  powerful io layer under the hood  um and just to give you an idea of what  this would look like  three parallel read tasks from s3 you  can feed your data to the trainers  pretty quickly  um also like how exactly do we keep the  gpu from sitting up idle how do we keep  the gpu saturated uh with data sets we  use the pipelining of the pre-processing  and shuffling stages with training  and uh instead of having these like  little gaps of gpu idle time you will  instead see that by pipelining the  shuffle data loading stage with the  model training stage we are able to  limit that gpu idle time just to pretty  much the first epoch after that first  epoch uh since we're concurrently uh  loading and shuffling the data uh for  the next epoch while training on the  kearney epoch uh you'll see that uh the  trainers never really have to wait for  the data loading and shuffling to finish  and as long as your shuffle data loading  throughput is uh is more than your  training throughput  then uh basically after the first epoch  the trainer should only see hot shuffle  data um and then per epoch shuffling  it's a difficult distributed problem how  do we do it efficiently well as alex  talked about our great distributed in  memory object store allows us to do  super efficient transfers and also zero  copy reads for workers that are on the  same node it allows us to do these  shuffle operations the map and reduce  stages very quickly  we also have locality replacement of  shuffle shards onto trainers uh and  that's as simple in this api example uh  where we give these locality hints where  we say like hey these are all of the  trainers  do your best when splitting the data set  into shards for uh one shard for each  trainer do your best to co-locate those  on the same node um so you don't have to  transfer data over to the network so  it's just small  optimizations like that that can make a  huge difference uh in like your actual  shuffled data loading throughput  which of course affects your training  throughput and so here is an example  there of  what this  this little uh workflow translates to  in terms of the pipelining uh the repeat  of the random shuffle on each epoch and  the eventual split of the shards to the  training workers  and then as alex also talked about um we  use windowing in order to limit the  working set size  for uh for a shuffled ingestion pipeline  and this prevents us from running out of  cluster memory and prevents us from  spilling through the disk so as you can  see here we're only  doing our shuffle data loading on one  window at a time  um so despite us dividing the data set  in the three windows here and limiting  the size of that intermediate data set  that's in the cluster just at one time  this is all still just one epoch  here you are trading off shuffle quality  with cluster size  by windowing you can have a smaller  cluster since the working set size is  smaller but you're going to no longer be  shuffling on the entire global data set  at once you're only shuffling on smaller  windows so your shuffle quality takes a  little bit of a hit  and uh this we also keep the api very  simple i'm gonna go through this a  little bit quickly since we're limited  on time the trainer api here is just  like a simple task that consumes a shard  um and then trains on gpu batches  and this is what you would probably have  right here this code example for a  ingestion pipeline on small data and  when training on a single node um so  relatively simple you basically read  parque you repeat for the number of  epochs that you want you should make  sure that each window is shuffled and  then you feed that data set to the  trainer for it to consume and train on  to extend this to a large data scale and  a multi-node distributed training  setting this is really all that's  required right here  you window to constrain the working set  size and you split the data into shards  and feed those uh to trainers  um so case studies uh first a little bit  of background common problem here is  like the existing solution as a  bottleneck in their training pipeline um  so this is where shuffled data loading  is really the issue why they're not  training as fast as they want and then  also the models are shuffle sensitive  where the shuffle quality does in fact  affect model accuracy  we have two case studies here a  high-tech ml platform that's a startup  that's currently in stealth mode their  old solution was actually writing data  to s3 and then using petastorm to load  it into horabad  for those that don't know pedostorm is  an ingest library from uber  our solution that we pitched was porting  the pandas uh  to s3 uh portion of the pipeline to das  unrave preprocessing and then feeding  that directly to raid data sets for  ingestion into the horva trainers  and then the other case study was a  large tech company in transport space  that was originally loading data from s3  again using petastorm and we actually  said continue loading data from s3 but  this time use data sets uh both case  studies suffered from the above common  problems by the way like both of these  have the same issues  um so uh for benchmark results uh very  quick summary uh ray uh desk and raiden  datasets was eight times faster than the  old solution on a popular new york city  taxi data set and also uh for the other  case study of where we're still loading  from s3 uh we were four times faster  and this is by the way uh when we're  doing more work because we're doing a  global shuffle instead of a local  shuffle so you're getting a higher  quality shuffle and better performance  even at small scales  and then we're also working on more  comprehensive benchmarks that are in  progress  um the other use case i'll go over very  quickly is batch inference where you do  some pre-processing and then you perform  model inference over uh batches of data  and save the results  um so there are a few issues here there  are some scheduling issues you don't  want to transfer the data around the  cluster and you want to reserve gps for  a fraction of the time and you need to  keep the gpu saturated as we talked  about before  um we saw the scheduling issue with  raise grade scheduling properties  automatic data locality and raised  resource scheduling  and uh we use pipelining to solve the  issue of idle gpus  and the  case studies we have a startup that's  processing drone imagery and a high-tech  ml platform that's uh got an existing  solution uh just using pandas to torch  and the results  uh just to skip through it we're five  times faster on this data set for batch  entrance  um as you can see from these graphs here  and so we provide more resource  efficiency and better performance even  at small data scales  uh so in summary existing ml platforms  our ml pipelines suffer from a lot of  issues and raid data sets  we hit that mark  using uh hyperscale implementations of  ml operations like shuffled um we keep  the api simple and for mlm.js and batch  inference we  beat the existing competition by a lot  in terms of performance  so yeah i had to go through the end  there pretty quickly but uh yeah thank  you for listening to the talk and then  uh we're probably a little bit out of  time for questions but please hit us up  on our discussion forum uh check us out  online and uh we're hiring so if these  problems are interesting you please come  check us out
 

